    (6, "System", "Check before building", "New rule in CLAUDE.md and the kalshi skill: read the market's own rules and write its fact card first; every plan lists what is verified and what is assumed.", "verified vs assumed", "Current", ""),
    (6, "Results", "Market-maker equation v0 (all 6)", "First market-maker equation on all 6 markets, scored on events it never saw. Center: explains 79% (diesel daily), 71% (gas daily), 68% (diesel weekly), 72% (gas weekly), 29% (gas monthly); diesel monthly has too few events. Daily center is about 0.7x yesterday's AAA move with almost no futures. Width: not cracked (simple time model fails in 5 of 6). Shape: a bell curve misses each line by about 3 price points everywhere, and long shots are priced 2-3 points richer in weekly and monthly.", "center ok, width no, shape close", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Results", "Width layer (B) on all 6", "Tried 4 forms for the crowd's range (width). Cracked only for gas weekly: 55% explained on weeks it never saw (error 43% vs 69% with no model). Gas daily is half way (error 47% vs 83%). Diesel daily, diesel weekly and gas monthly fail on later dates, yet all fit fine when eras are mixed (52-67%). So the form is right but its numbers move over time.", "1 of 6 cracked", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Results", "How the 6 widths differ", "Range one day out: about 1c in daily and gas weekly, 1.7c gas monthly, 2.4c diesel weekly. A week out: 2c daily, 4.6c gas weekly, 6-7c gas monthly and diesel weekly. Daily ranges barely grow with time (power 0.35); weekly and monthly grow fast (0.6-0.8). Daily ranges are about 2 lines wide, monthly gas only a third of one line, so monthly width is fuzzy to measure.", "daily flat, long ones grow", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Results", "Crowd's range is too wide", "In all 6 markets the final AAA number lands inside the crowd's own range more often than a well-sized range would: 78-88% of the time vs 68%. The crowd is less sure than it should be. This is a hint, not a tested trade: costs are not in it.", "78-88% vs 68%", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Verdict", "Gas monthly center not solved", "Gas monthly center is still not solved. Three forms (plain, slow pass-through, scale-free) explain only 19-28% on later months, and 38% with eras mixed. Cause: the market itself changed in 2026 (16-44 lines vs 4-11, and expected moves 2.5x larger), so old months do not teach the new ones. Only 29 events. Diesel monthly has 3 events: cannot be fit yet.", "era drift, 29 events", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Results", "Anchor engine (big data first)", "New build: the pump-price engine is fit on outside data only (about 1,050 weeks of EIA pump vs wholesale prices since 2006, plus 500-630 days of AAA), then Kalshi sets just 2 dials per market. Center on events never seen: diesel daily 84% (was 76%), diesel weekly 76% (was 66%), gas weekly 68%, gas daily 63% (was 70%), gas monthly 32% (was 28%). Range: gas weekly 59%, gas monthly 40% (was -4%); both daily markets and diesel weekly still fail. Diesel monthly: 3 events, dials borrowed from gas, not proven.", "2 dials, 5 of 6 scored", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Verdict", "Copy is not close enough in price yet", "In price terms the copy is still far off: each line is 6-16 price points from the real price, while the bid-ask gap is 2-4 points. Only 4-18% of lines land inside the real bid-ask. It beats a no-model copy (8-26 points) but is not a usable baseline yet. On half-cent ladders a half-cent center miss moves a line about 15 points.", "6-16 pts vs 2-4 spread", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Verdict", "Crowd sees something we do not", "At the 8 AM open the daily crowd is already within 0.3-0.4c of the next AAA number. Our best engine is 0.6-0.7c off; plain yesterday's-move momentum is 0.7c. So the crowd holds an input worth about half our miss from the first minute. Finding that input is the shortest path to a baseline copy (north star: unexplained move = missing input).", "0.35c vs 0.65c at open", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Verdict", "8 AM input hunt: not found in history", "Backtested 3 suspects that have history against the crowd's opening number (40 diesel + 98 gas days never seen). Miss to the next AAA number: yesterday's move only 0.81c diesel / 0.62c gas; + last 7 moves 0.76 / 0.56; + day of week 0.62 / 0.40; + wholesale 0.44 / 0.46. Crowd: 0.25 / 0.25. None is the missing input. Day of week is real and was missing from our engine.", "best 0.40-0.44c vs crowd 0.25c", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Verdict", "Crowd nearly knows the answer by midnight", "By the close the crowd's center explains 98-99% of the next AAA move (miss 0.12-0.13c), hours before AAA posts at about 3:20 AM. Its miss shrinks through the day, mostly between noon and 6 PM. That is the footprint of a live station-price feed (GasBuddy or OPIS type) that runs ahead of AAA. It has no free history, so it cannot be backtested with data we hold today.", "98-99% by close", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Data", "Futures pull was monthly, now fixed + curve", "Found while checking: the full-history futures files held one bar per MONTH, not per day (Yahoo shrinks them when asked for max range). Fixed to real daily bars. Also added the next 4 contract months of gas and diesel futures (the curve), needed for monthly ladders. The master was not hurt: it reads futures from EIA and the project databases.", "daily bars + 4 months out", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Data", "Slice table + ratio layer built", "New layers on top of the master, all 6 markets: 23,757 rows (market x event x time x busy line) over 542 events, each with the favorite side (priced above 50%), cost with fee, outcome label (favorite won as Yes / won as No / lost as Yes / lost as No), return per $ for favorite and underdog, plus 11 of our own ratios and 4 labels. File: data/lake/master/slices.csv.gz. Tool: tools/slice_scan.py.", "23,757 rows, 11 ratios", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Results", "Underdogs lose everywhere", "Backing the underdog on the busy lines loses 33% to 58% per $ after costs in all 6 markets, in both the early and late blocks. Long shots are overpriced. Favorites win 76-90% of the time but, after costs, only about break even overall (diesel +4%, gas about 0).", "dogs -33% to -58% per $", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Results", "Slice scan: 18 leads, none proven", "1,328 slices cut, 660 big enough to test (20+ events), 18 pass the false-winner filter early AND stay positive late. Top leads: gas daily No-favorites at night, +3% to +16% per $ early and +3% to +10% late (35-91 late events); diesel daily Yes-favorites at 90c+ in the morning, 37 of 37 then 28 of 28 wins, +3-5% per $; diesel daily favorites when the crowd's range is narrow vs recent jumpiness, +9-10%. Late-block strength is modest (most t under 2), and about this many survivors is what luck alone can give. Leads for a forward test, not proof.", "18 of 660, leads only", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Verdict", "Long markets too thin to slice yet", "Diesel weekly (15 events), gas monthly (33) and diesel monthly (3) had zero slices big enough to test. Gas weekly gave 1 survivor. One hint: gas weekly Yes-favorites lost 25% then 17% per $. More history, not more slicing, is what these markets need.", "0 testable in 3 of 6", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Results", "Favorite ledger: gas crowd leans too high", "Plain count of every favorite on the 3 busy lines (23,757 instances, 542 events), in 4 boxes by price band. In gas, No-favorites win more often than their price says and Yes-favorites win less: gas daily No +3.3 points of edge vs Yes -4.6; gas weekly No +4.8 vs Yes -6.3; gas monthly No +2.5 vs Yes -4.4. Same sign in early and late events, in every year 2023-2026, and in falling, flat and rising stretches. Diesel does not show it (both sides about +3). Strongest boxes: gas weekly No at 60-80c (+9 to +13 points, 45-49 late events), diesel daily No at 60-80c (+10 to +12). Lead, not proof: needs a forward test and real fill prices. File: research/engine_room/favorite_ledger.csv; sheet tab Favorite Ledger.", "gas: No +3 to +5, Yes -4 to -6", "Open", ""),
    (6, "Results", "Ledger by time slot and line", "Same count cut by the 7 time slots and by busy-line rank. Gas daily No-favorites: edge grows through the day, from -1.5 points at the open to +7.8 at the close; gas daily Yes-favorites are negative at every slot. Gas weekly No-favorites: about +4.5 at every slot. Busy-line rank 1, 2 or 3 makes little difference. File: research/engine_room/favorite_ledger_by_time_and_line.csv.", "gas No: -1.5 open to +7.8 close", "Open", ""),
    (6, "System", "Spine: one map that proves the tables connect", "New data/lake/master/SPINE.md (tools/spine.py): every research table with its layer (L0 inputs to L4 market-maker copy), its keys (market, event, time slot, line), which engine it serves (E1 hold to settle, E2 intraday price path) and join checks. All joins pass at 100% once times are compared as times. All tables are now in the database layer (tools/warehouse.py, schema lab). Sheets stay read-only copies. Three small builder issues found and listed: two timestamp text styles; clock marks one hour early on the 2 days a year clocks change (60 rows); open landing on a clock mark counted twice (0.6% of slice rows).", "layers L0-L4, joins 100%", "Current", ""),
    (7, "Strategy", "Three tracks of edge", "Every finding and test is one of three kinds: A = structure (the crowd's habits), B = information (knowing AAA sooner), C = path (the price route during the day). Each needs its own data and pass mark.", "A habits / B know sooner / C path", "Current", ""),
    (7, "System", "Canvas locked: master versioned", "The 3 builder bugs are fixed and the master table has a version stamp (m20261128-0345b9a1). Every result names the version it read. Spine checks are all 100% and run after every rebuild.", "VERSION.json", "Current", ""),
    (7, "System", "Market scorecard", "The six markets side by side on one ruler: events, favorite edge vs its noise, cost, crowd miss, taker vs maker, depth. Three can be tested now (gas daily, gas weekly, diesel daily); three have too few events.", "research/engine_room/market_scorecard.csv", "Current", ""),
    (7, "System", "Question log + test command", "Quick questions on data we already hold get a dated entry in research/QUESTION_LOG.md and in the Google Sheet Kalshi - Question Log. Answer tool: tools/ask.py. A finding there is a look-back until it holds on new days.", "test <question>", "Current", ""),
    (7, "Rules", "Guard rails", "About 3,300 cuts were run before the log; each question adds one. Baselines to beat: the crowd's price, no change, skip long shots. Vault = events settling after Nov 27, 2026, never looked at.", "trial count + baselines + vault", "Current", ""),
    (7, "Results", "Favorites 75c+ (each event counted once)", "They win about 9 in 10 in every market. That beats the price paid only in diesel daily: 95.3% vs 92.7% needed, +2.6 points, noise 2.0, 110 events from one falling stretch. Gas is priced fair. Look-back.", "win % vs break-even", "Open", ""),
    (7, "Results", "Gas No-favorite lead shrinks", "Counting every row, gas daily No-favorites were +3.5 points. Counting each event once they are about +1, inside the noise. The firmer gas finding is the other side: Yes-favorites lose (daily -3.3, weekly -10.2).", "rows vs events", "Open", ""),
    (7, "Results", "Takers beat makers in our two daily markets", "Before fees: diesel takers +5.6% per $ vs makers -6.8% (61 days); gas +2.7% vs -1.9% (31 days). Kalshi overall is the reverse. Short tape; diesel just beyond noise, gas inside it.", "taker > maker here", "Open", ""),
    (7, "Data", "Becker public data set", "All Kalshi markets and trades, Jun 2021 to Nov 2025, 36 GiB, same fields we already pull (no order book). Ends before diesel existed. Use: gas trade tape 2023-2025 and an all-Kalshi baseline. Its host is blocked from the Claude sandbox.", "Jon-Becker/prediction-market-analysis", "Open", ""),
    (7, "Data", "Enough data? Map yes, proof no", "The full bet grid has 10,748 cells; 2,424 have 30+ events and only 233 can tell a 3-point edge from luck. A typical readable cell only sees edges above about 6 points. Diesel weekly and monthly: zero readable cells.", "23% readable / 2% sharp", "Current", ""),
    (7, "Results", "Bet grid: losers are real, winners are not proven", "1,000 replays of a fair market: 276 losing cells vs 52 by luck (2 replays in 1,000 match), 77 winning cells vs 44 by luck (131 in 1,000 match). Buying YES is overpriced in gas at almost every price; long shots lose everywhere.", "losers 276 vs 52 / winners 77 vs 44", "Current", ""),
    (7, "Results", "Candidate: gas daily NO-favorite late evening", "Late in the evening the crowd ladder sits about 0.05c (10 PM) to 0.10c (close) too high; with lines half a cent apart that is worth 5 to 9 points. $100 a night on NO-favorites at 10 PM: 200 nights, 85.5% winning nights, +$1,592, about +$8 a night (noise 5). November negative. Look-back; no size data for late quotes.", "NO at 10 PM-close", "Open", ""),
